45585] [17592186130056 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130057 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130056 #edb/ref 1061 23134 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130056 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "773e70e4-737a-5fa4-92ed-0e1d04815ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130056 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130391 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130057 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130055 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130057 #edb/ref 1061 23135 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca4b822d-4434-5d75-8400-5106f71e78a9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129186 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130058 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130073 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130058 #edb/ref 1061 23153 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130058 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0355e3e0-f89b-5dca-97bc-21791fa4aed2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130058 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130392 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130073 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130052 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130073 #edb/ref 1061 25668 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130073 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0296b5b5-c2ce-56c6-a8e4-ad0af71e0dde" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130073 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130402 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130388 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{'}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{'}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{'}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130388 #edb/ref 1065 3914 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130388 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48f4e466-1052-5e98-b9a0-d345e3139cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130388 #edb/ref 1067 "Defining the Differentiability of Single Variable Functions in Terms of Tangent Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130389 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0,y_0).$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nAssume that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables. Since $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0),$ then there exists a vector $\\nabla f = \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (x_0,y_0)}\\dfrac {f(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle} {||\\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle||} = 0. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFor the above limit to hold, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $(x_0,y_0),$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) \\approx \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nExpanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\cdot f_1+ (y-y_0)\\cdot f_2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to show that $f_1$ and $f_2$ are the partial derivatives.\n\n- Let $y=y_0$ in the first equation $(\\ast)$ above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x,y_0) - f(x_0,y_0) - (x-x_0) f_1} {x-x_0} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is the definition of the partial derivative with respect to $x.$ So, we have $f_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Similarly, letting $x = x_0$ gives $f_2 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ can be linearized as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\\approx f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nBy setting $z=f(x,y)$ and $z_0 = f(x_0,y_0),$ we get the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0, y_0).$\n\nFinally, note that we have also shown that \n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\nabla f$ is known as the **gradient vector**." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130389 #edb/ref 1065 3915 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130389 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3e6cb319-460f-5a22-888e-e3f886ea9b0b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130389 #edb/ref 1067 "Defining the Differentiability of Multivariable Functions in Terms of Tangent Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130390 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = f(x,y)$ at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be given as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nSlightly rewriting the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (x-x_0) + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (y-y_0) + ({\\color{blue}-1}) \\cdot (z - z_0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich immediately gives us a normal vector to the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}-1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the tangent plane to the surface is called a **normal vector to the surface**." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130390 #edb/ref 1065 3919 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130390 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5ebd9d6-c219-583a-8bca-87268b8aa8bc" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130390 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Normal Vector to a Surface" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130391 #edb/ref 1064 "Until now, we considered only functions of the form \n\n![](images/48/4838bea41cd622ef69be5caaafac89df818d35cf108730686d68d7b7a12265ac.svg)\n\nof two independent variables. But what about the case of functions \n\n![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg)\n\nwith three variables? Can we define a tangent plane to such a function?\n\nThe answer is yes! Analogously to the case with two variables, a function of three variables ![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg) is differentiable at ![](images/f4/f46650c560f32308db30c404d86da2d5921654805cca770cd2bc3627e55dbf86.svg) if it admits a **unique tangent hyperplane** at ![](images/3f/3fe19998bf33d149c699a01ca363c2691041b3ecfd52c47a167053b4097dbb57.svg) where ![](images/7e/7e2db5c48c69709e3c1d04d4834af7cc0b2ff0df186cf1713e207c164a8e41e5.svg) The equation of the **tangent hyperplane** is given by\n\n![](images/f4/f433839875810dc7417400a22bad7b8a63c0eb1033954a8e91e7049f3ada361a.svg)\n\nNotice that we use the term *hyperplane*. The graphs of functions of three variables live in four-dimensional space. The equation above describes an object (a so-called hyperplane) in four-dimensional space that can be considered an analog to a *plane* in three-dimensional space." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130391 #edb/ref 1065 3920 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130391 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d6f087f4-e5d4-59f8-90f7-a5a9484a0c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130391 #edb/ref 1067 "The Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130392 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130392 #edb/ref 1065 3921 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130392 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bced0695-3ebd-5b62-b7ff-9c8f2500fc2a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130392 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1064 "A surface $z = f(x,y)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent plane at $P.$ Intuitively, this means that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the surface $z=f(x,y)$ can be approximated as a plane.\n\nNote that this is analogous to the case of functions of one variable. A function $y = f(x)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent *line* at $P.$ This is the same as saying that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the curve $y=f(x)$ can be approximated as a straight line.  \n\nWe'll explore these ideas in more depth at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at computing tangent planes." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1065 4231 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "015c4e8e-c1ba-5672-8228-ff0869681d6d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130402 #edb/ref 1067 "A Note on Differentiability" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263630 #edb/ref 1081 "(-1,-1,4)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263630 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263630 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "4831fde7-328b-5f95-8b4f-bd555404f79f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263795 #edb/ref 1081 "(1,0,3)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263795 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263795 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "5b9d3b86-7aec-5970-92c9-47d3d544786f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264280 #edb/ref 1081 "(0,1,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264280 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264280 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "94d3b3cb-73a3-5f94-abc9-28adc6612ba0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264351 #edb/ref 1081 "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264351 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264351 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264421 #edb/ref 1081 "(-\\frac{1}{3},0,\\frac{1}{9})" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264421 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264421 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "a54e3749-cf7d-5ffc-ac93-9256ee36c08f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263630 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263795 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264280 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264351 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264421 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186264351 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265354 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "38b8a09a-7de4-5c87-b2a5-460a354244c2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265754 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265354 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265754 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129557" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265754 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265754 #edb/ref 1071 "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265754 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12830aeb-cb43-530e-902c-e52ba9d7524f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265754 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6cx=4 \\\\ 2cy=-2 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186321709 #edb/ref 1081 "(2\\pi ,-\\pi ,2\\pi )" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186321709 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186321709 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "464dff10-0c59-5dad-80a1-2ee16d60dbfa" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186321721 #edb/ref 1081 "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186321721 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186321721 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "48223b68-6d40-5d37-8bb5-b27364a9cf80" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322433 #edb/ref 1081 "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322433 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322433 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "a226c677-0c51-577f-b0f5-1b3064cc6815" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322518 #edb/ref 1081 "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,12\\pi )" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322518 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322518 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "adf98dbd-c12a-5550-9749-ddcc015f00fd" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322842 #edb/ref 1081 "(-2,6,8\\pi )" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322842 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186322842 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "d80a3e3a-b37d-53fa-b4ba-7e5d51839fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321709 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321721 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322433 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322518 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322842 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186322433 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323357 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "5baf0726-2cff-5a6b-904f-2992e085ab37" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323357 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37986" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323970 #edb/ref 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c9333a9-b5d6-5ec8-b1f3-0c62768fb8b7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186323970 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy=8 \\\\ -xc=2\\pi  \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186339887 #edb/ref 1081 "$(-1,3,28)$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186339887 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186339887 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "52002c6b-9859-5b7b-a1de-586418b14736" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186339916 #edb/ref 1081 "$(1,-3,0)$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [1759