 to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ at the point $(1,1,\\pi ,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de6bd964-56b9-511c-9d67-ed8d06732a08" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342099 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi )+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi )+(z-\\pi )\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi (x-1)-\\pi (y-1)-(z-\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi x+\\pi -\\pi y+\\pi -z+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341793 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73857" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342103 #edb/ref 1071 "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c589653c-432a-5bb8-acb2-94698429fbb4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186342103 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407546 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407547 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407548 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407549 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407550 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407538 #edb/ref 1045 "Tange